{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"99e22285e010d092","name":"card_event_act_on_the_intel","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Poland/events/card_event_act_on_the_intel","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"ACT ON THE INTEL","text":"Deal damage to target enemy equal to known cards in the enemy hand.","flavorText":"A notable example of how the Polish acted on the intelligence gathered during World War II is the Warsaw Uprising of 1944. The Polish underground resistance, known as the Home Army (Armia Krajowa), collected extensive intelligence on German military activities and plans in occupied Warsaw. With the intelligence indicating that the Soviet Red Army was approaching Warsaw, the Home Army decided to launch an uprising against the German occupiers on August 1, 1944. The uprising aimed to liberate the city and establish Polish authority before the Soviets arrived. The intelligence gathered by the Home Army helped in identifying German troop movements, supply routes, and defensive positions. This information allowed the Polish resistance fighters to plan targeted attacks, sabotage operations, and coordinated offensives against German forces. The intelligence also aided in the establishment of a makeshift radio network, which enabled communication between different units and with the outside world. This allowed the Home Army to relay valuable information about the situation in Warsaw to the Allies and the Polish government-in-exile. Although the Warsaw Uprising ultimately faced overwhelming German military power and Soviet reluctance to provide direct support, the intelligence gathered by the Polish underground played a crucial role in the initial stages of the uprising. It allowed the Home Army to launch surprise attacks, disrupt German supply lines, and control key strategic points within the city for a significant period of time.","faction":"Poland","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":6,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_act_on_the_intel","title":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_act_on_the_intel_title","SourceString":"ACT ON THE INTEL","LocalizedString":"ACT ON THE INTEL"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Poland","kredits":6,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_act_on_the_intel_text","SourceString":"Deal damage to target enemy equal to known cards in the enemy hand.","LocalizedString":"Deal damage to target enemy equal to known cards in the enemy hand."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_act_on_the_intel_history","SourceString":"A notable example of how the Polish acted on the intelligence gathered during World War II is the Warsaw Uprising of 1944. The Polish underground resistance, known as the Home Army (Armia Krajowa), collected extensive intelligence on German military activities and plans in occupied Warsaw. With the intelligence indicating that the Soviet Red Army was approaching Warsaw, the Home Army decided to launch an uprising against the German occupiers on August 1, 1944. The uprising aimed to liberate the city and establish Polish authority before the Soviets arrived. The intelligence gathered by the Home Army helped in identifying German troop movements, supply routes, and defensive positions. This information allowed the Polish resistance fighters to plan targeted attacks, sabotage operations, and coordinated offensives against German forces. The intelligence also aided in the establishment of a makeshift radio network, which enabled communication between different units and with the outside world. This allowed the Home Army to relay valuable information about the situation in Warsaw to the Allies and the Polish government-in-exile. Although the Warsaw Uprising ultimately faced overwhelming German military power and Soviet reluctance to provide direct support, the intelligence gathered by the Polish underground played a crucial role in the initial stages of the uprising. It allowed the Home Army to launch surprise attacks, disrupt German supply lines, and control key strategic points within the city for a significant period of time.","LocalizedString":"A notable example of how the Polish acted on the intelligence gathered during World War II is the Warsaw Uprising of 1944. The Polish underground resistance, known as the Home Army (Armia Krajowa), collected extensive intelligence on German military activities and plans in occupied Warsaw. With the intelligence indicating that the Soviet Red Army was approaching Warsaw, the Home Army decided to launch an uprising against the German occupiers on August 1, 1944. The uprising aimed to liberate the city and establish Polish authority before the Soviets arrived. The intelligence gathered by the Home Army helped in identifying German troop movements, supply routes, and defensive positions. This information allowed the Polish resistance fighters to plan targeted attacks, sabotage operations, and coordinated offensives against German forces. The intelligence also aided in the establishment of a makeshift radio network, which enabled communication between different units and with the outside world. This allowed the Home Army to relay valuable information about the situation in Warsaw to the Allies and the Polish government-in-exile. Although the Warsaw Uprising ultimately faced overwhelming German military power and Soviet reluctance to provide direct support, the intelligence gathered by the Polish underground played a crucial role in the initial stages of the uprising. It allowed the Home Army to launch surprise attacks, disrupt German supply lines, and control key strategic points within the city for a significant period of time."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BrothersInArms","isReserved":true,"usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnIntelTriggered"],"cipher":3,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Poland/Spring2023Candidates/t_act_on_the_intel.t_act_on_the_intel","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Jarosław Wróbel","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/ACT_ON_THE_INTEL.ACT_ON_THE_INTEL","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":447.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_act_on_the_intel_photo","SourceString":"A Polish resistance fighter in a Warsaw tramway driver uniform carries a K pattern flamethrower that was produced by the Polish Home Army.","LocalizedString":"A Polish resistance fighter in a Warsaw tramway driver uniform carries a K pattern flamethrower that was produced by the Polish Home Army."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"damage","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"allHandCardsAfter","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"allUnseenCardsAfter","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_act_on_the_intel_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flags":"FUNC_Event | FUNC_Public | FUNC_HasOutParms | FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[{"name":"CallFunc_GetOppositeSide_oppositeSide","type":"ESideEnum","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_GetTargetedCard_hasTarget","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_GetTargetedCard_card","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_EqualEqual_ByteByte_ReturnValue","type